From: timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr)
Date: Fri, 25 Mar 1994 04:17:25 -0800
Subject: [#94-3-284] Ozone Depletion

Geoff Dale notes the questionability of the science underlying the theory
of ozone depletion.  What's truly astounding to me is how much trouble the
physicists and chemists and atmospheric scientists go to to argue that CFCs
result in ozone depletion, compared to how little attention is given to
the biological effects of it.  Even if you assume that CFCs are depleting
the ozone, the question of how much of a hazard to public health and to
agriculture, etc., arises.  And, as with car pollution, that's where the
science is weakest.  Increased surface UV radiation has actually been
shown beneficial to some plants, and the greatest public health hazard is
that it will increase the incidence of skin cancer - not the really dangerous
kind, but the more benign and 98% curable kind, with the other 2% attributable
to lack of diagnosis.  Further, the level of increase would be equivalent to
moving 60 miles closer to the equator...

The debate over whether CFCs actually do cause ozone depletion can get very
long and involved, with people challenging such unverifiable things as
whether atmospheric ozone comes from CFCs or volcanoes, but when you realize
how trivial the "risks" are (the "risk" that plants will grow better?)...

It becomes clear that the whole thing is a bunch of exagerrated nonsense
cooked up by researchers to keep the grant money coming.

