From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Date: Fri, 25 Mar 1994 06:44:42 -0600 (CST)
Subject: [#94-3-286] Grigsby's Fallacy

Tim Starr writes:

> >...I remember a statement to the effect of "Automobiles don't 
> >pollute enough to infringe on your right to breathable air".
> 
> That was probably mine, and what I maintain is that there is no proof that
> car pollution is a significant threat to public health.

That is the difficult problem with these kinds of issues.  Anybody
whose point of view wants them to conclude that something isn't
harmful is free to do so (for example, the cigarette industry
continues to maintain that there is no proof that smoking cigarettes
is a significant threat to health).

I'm not claiming that automobiles are a threat to public health
(though I suspect that they are unless the unhealthy smog-ridden
areas of the world just coincidentally coincide with the automobile-
infested areas) -- the important question is how such issues get
resolved.

As I understand the anarchocapitalist solution to this problem,
somebody harmed by auto emissions or pollution or whatever would
have to individually demonstrate harm done by each individual
polluter, a vast and impossible task.

I would very much like to see this problem resolved; to me, it's
the only unresolved issue (me having been convinced that personal
violence can be adequately handled by a-c institutions) and the one
that turns people off the most.

The most common defense I see is a head-in-the-sand approach, where
it is implied that there have never been, are not now, and will never
be, such problems -- as in:  Ozone hole, who cares?  Cars don't pollute!
Groundwater's healthier than it's ever been!  Rainforests are clearly
unnecessary for a stable ecology! ...  therefore it is unnecessary to
address the issue of how they can be solved.

Personally, though I find such arguments unconvincing (as I think do
many people, which is a source of this 'jamal' person's self-righteous
indignation), I am hopeful that there will be overwhelming technological
solutions to these problems before they reach a critical level.

Which is a different, but more optimistic, head-in-the-sand approach.

But, on the other hand, I don't really see fairly modest regulation
being very harmful to the development of those technological solutions
(as long as it stays fairly modest).  2% of cars in california have to
be electric by 2000 or something like that.  Despite my distaste for
such meddling, it's hard for me to get too upset about it; the induced
inefficiency is fairly minimal.

In fact, sometimes I wonder whether "statist" policies are in fact
less efficient for the production of things like nanotechnology.
My understanding is that Japan's explicitly centralized approach
has taken them much farther than our less centralized industry has
on its own.  Note that I do not advocate statist approaches to
nanotechnology:  effectiveness even if demonstrated is insufficient
justification for tyranny.

derek

