   I think the problem with this analysis is that it assumes human
cooperation is a linear effect. That is, organized humans do not produce
more "order" than the information required to specify the group. You
are basically saying that the "extropy" of a social group is merely
proportional to the number of humans in it. I think this is a bad
measure, unfortunately, at the moment, I can't think of a better one.

> >  Ok, think of the system of particles that makes up the brain as a
> >blackbox.
> >                     +-----------------+
> >Sensory Input ---->  | brain           | ----> output (move an arm, etc)
> >                     +-----------------+
> >
> >  Now, if there exists a machine which can simulate the particles in
> >the box perfectly and its predictions are never wrong, then we can replace
> >the box with a simulation.
> 
> If mechanistic theories of consciousness are true...

   The negation meaning that consciousness is not subject to any natural law
thus placing consciousness in the realm of the supernatural. More on
this later.

> > [...] If for some reason, there is
> >a "ghost" in the machine which causes the simulation to diverge from 
> >the output of the "real" box of particles, then the simulation is not
> >perfect. At this point, either 1 of 2 things are true:
> >1) the effect of the ghost is minimal and there is a natural law which
> >governs it [thus we can discover it and tweak the simulation]
> >2) the effect of the ghost has no pattern we can discover whatsoever
> >(in this case, it is totally random and can't be predicted)
> 
> 2) sounds the more likely to me.

  Ok, #2 posits a phenomena somewhere in the brain which takes a chemical
brain from being "non-conscious" into one that is "conscious" If this
phenomena were patterned in any way, it could be further simplified
and built into the simulation. On the other hand, if from observation,
we can discover no pattern in this ghost at all, no natural "law" which
governs its behavior (no matter how complex that law may be), then
the data set from observation is incompressible, and it follows
from algorithmic information theory that this ghost is also
*completely random* At this point, I must question the usefulness
of a ghost which is completely random *yet* produces orderly human
behavior/consciousness.

  Now I can imagine a middle ground. What if this ghost is indeed
orderly and predictable, only God has played a cruel joke. When the
ghost interacts with the real material brain, its effects are first filtered 
through a *crytographically strong* cipher, and then the function of the
material brain is simply to decrypt these instructions and have the body
carry them out. Given this situation, mankind may search in vain to 
simulate the brain but will always fall prey to a random component
(the observed encrypted signal. consider the brain's neural network
just a really complex stream cipher decryptor) I find this very
unlikely, but plausible. Still, in principle it would be possible to 
decode the encryption just like it will be possible to break a tamper
proof clipper chip.

  This thought experment still doesn't refute my claim: a perfect simulation
is as good as the real thing. If a ghost exists it is either 1)
totally random so we can't in principle represent it finitely in some
machine or 2) it is not random, and some encoding exists which can
simulate it perfectly. If #1 is the situation, the ghost's behavior
is as useful as flipping a coin.

