From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Mar 1994 12:36:37 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-3-298] PCR: certainty of materialism

On Sat, 26 Mar 1994, Stephen J. Whitrow wrote:

> We know that "there is a material world" is, at least, an extremely high
> credibility belief.  I was interested in whether it would be classed as
> a certainty in PCR terms, and it seems to me that it could for these
> reasons: 1) It could never be disproved. 2) It wipes the floor with
> varieties of idealism on grounds of credibility: predictive capability /
> explanatory power and utility.

Well, it's a certainty as far as I'm concerned. But several people around
here use the word "certainty" very differently from the way I do. 

I think the big one here is Explanatory Power. This is a criticism that 
Bartley doesn't discuss very explicitly in TRTC, though his Check of the 
Problem (does the idea actually solve the problem it was advanced to 
address?) is something like a generalization of the Explanatory Power 
criticism. Churchland, in MATTER AND CONSCIOUSNESS points out eloquently, 
clearly, and thoroughly how mind-matter dualism utterly fails the 
criticism of explanatory power. Like the idea of God, the idea of the 
dualistic mindstuff doesn't *explain* anything; it just packages up all 
the mysteries and seals them off from physical examination (and in the 
case of the pietist's God, even from rational analysis). Both these 
"explanations" are worse than no explanation at all.

> Actually, doesn't objective idealism win in terms of Ockham, since with
> the material world explained as "God's dream" the number of assumptions
> and entities are rather low?  Of course, since materialist explanations
> may include scientific theories of high credibility such as Darwinism and
> the laws of physics, they extinguish / smite Berkeley's God off the face
> of the universe.

I think I addressed these things in my recent post about objectivism. 
Berkeley was pretty careful to formulate his idealism in such terms that 
it was not inconsistent with scientific theory, but in so doing he had to 
postulate an objective sensory-field (perceived by God) that is no less 
complex (has no fewer entities) than the material world, and in 
*addition* to this, he has to postulate the all-perceiving God, which 
realist materialism does not require.

> Could PCR be expressed as a formula -- something like this?:
> 
> Credibility = [(3 * predictive power + Ockham rating + utility rating) / 5] 
> - [(possibility of disproof + 20000 * logical inconsistencies +
> 10000 * empirical contradictions) / 30001]

Well, yes, except for the problem that we have no numerical measure of
these things. But we also have no numerical measure to compare my utility
to your utility (though monetary prices can be a good approximation in
*some* contexts), and sometimes they do this sort of thing in theoretical
economics. Sometimes it's useful to think about the formulas even if you
don't have a way to measure for the numbers you would need to plug into
them. 

I still think PCR is more solidly based on the idea of refutation than of
credibility. Credibility is a guide to which ideas need to be refuted
(though the most rewarding refutations are those of highly credible ideas,
such as Einstein's refutation of Newton). If a PCRist finds an idea
extremely non-credible, and cannot refute or at least solidly criticize
it, that's a situation that should make the PCRist at least mildly
uncomfortable. 

PCR recognizes that proof is just a decomposition of a theorem into 
lemmas and simpler theorems, each of which themselves demand proof. 
Refutation shuts the door on infinite regress, but proof does not. We can 
achieve much more certainty about the falsity of a false idea than we can 
about the truth of a true idea. PCR exploits that strength, and seeks 
certainty through refutation rather than proof.

Refutation is simply the strongest variety of criticism.

I'm winging it here, so I hope I haven't said anything ridiculous in the 
last few paragraphs. It seemed like an interesting line of thought at the 
time.

> Here's a guide I used to use for judging the value of a belief:
> 
> 1) It must not conflict with the empirical evidence, and elements of the
> hypothesis (or grand unified theory) must be consistent with each other.
> 
> 2) It should motivate the individual to carry out strategies and behave in
> ways which are to his / her personal self-interest (the collective benefit of
> the species is also taken into account but in any case should be roughly
> aligned with self-interest -- especially for long-lived individuals).
> 
> 3) It should optimise the subjective mental states and self-esteem of the
> individual.
> 
> I think an extra condition could be added:
> 
> 4) It should not have "and then a miracle occurs" as part of the
> explanation.

Yeah. Miracles, like God and dualistic mindstuff, are 
explanation-postponers. They deal with mysteries by packaging them up and 
sealing them off. So 4) is similar to what I have been referring to as 
the criticism of explanatory power.

1) is the thing that nearly everyone agrees on. Though it's interesting to
point out (as Popper did in the Thirties) that the check of empirical
evidence relies on the check of logical consistency, and is not wholly
independent of it. If we call statements like "At 3:00 pm on Thursday,
rays of light from the star NGC3456778 were bent by the sun during a total
solar eclipse" 'basic statements' (Popper's term for them), then the check
of empirical evidence is a check for logical consistency between
potentially problematic scientific theories and nonproblematic basic
statements. Of course, if the experimental technique is contested, basic
statements can be problematic too. 

I am uncomfortable with your 3) and your 2) for a number of reasons. 
There is no question that value plays a role in knowledge-acquisition; 
after all, we seek to acquire knowledge *because* it is a value of ours,
even if only as a means. But the use of raw utility as a means of
distinguishing between true and false ideas is not the best because
different people have different values, yet I do not think that there are
multiple truths. If naked utility was a primary criterion for truth, as it
is in Orwell's 1984, you can get some very unpleasant situations indeed,
because we always have to ask "whose utility"? And again, I am also
uncomfortable because this feels imprecise. Certainly the check for
explanatory power is something of a judgement-call too, but isn't quite so
plagued by the multiplicity-of-values aspect. 

So the problem with using values as a check for truth or falsity is that
first you must address the question "Whose values?". Accordingly, I rely
on it as a check of last resort, for things such as noncontradiction for
which the "normal" checks would be mere question-begging. And even here it
is not reliable, as Chris Phoenix pointed out with his brainwashing
scenario. Noncontradiction was an idea explicitly rejected by the Oceanian
state in Orwell's novel; as a result, nearly everyone was miserable and
poor, but... 

So anyway, I would weight your 4) much more heavily than your 2) and your
3), though not as heavily as your 1). 

My apologies for not having time right now to condense this and make it
more readable. 

